<td id="S1.T1.2.1.19.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Qwen2.5-Omni (S<math id="S1.T1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>T)</td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.1.19.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">4.60</td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.1.19.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">3.98</td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.1.19.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">58.23</td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.1.19.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">61.51</td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.1.19.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">81.09</td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.1.19.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">53.33</td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.1.19.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">99.80</td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.1.19.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">75.08</td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To tackle these challenges, we conduct a systematic study with three main contributions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) General validation.</span> We perform the first systematic evaluation of multiple mainstream open-source speech LLMs regarding catastrophic forgetting and modality inequivalence. By feeding the same text queries to both a speech LLM and its corresponding text base model, we quantify the degradation of reasoning ability in text mode. Meanwhile, by comparing the outputs of speech queries and text queries on the same speech LLM, we assess inequivalence across modalities. Experiments confirm that these problems are widespread.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Cross-modal knowledge distillation.</span> We propose a novel strategy to jointly address forgetting and inequivalence. Inspired by cross-modal distillation in computer vision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, we treat the speech LLM as the student and its text LLM backbone (with stronger reasoning and knowledge capabilities) as the teacher. Distillation is performed in two stages: (a) <span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">text-to-text distillation</span>: using open instruction datasets such as OpenOrca <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, the speech LLM is trained to mimic the teacher’s outputs under text input, alleviating catastrophic forgetting; (b) <span id="S1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">speech-to-text distillation</span>: the same text data are converted to synthetic speech via TTS and fed to the speech LLM, while the teacher outputs remain the supervision signal, thereby strengthening semantic alignment across modalities. Prior works such as Desta2.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and MiDashengLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> explored leveraging text LLM knowledge via instruction tuning to enhance speech LLMs in acoustic audio analysis tasks. Their method demonstrated that text-based knowledge can improve audio modality performance in tasks such as “listing possible sound sources,” and “generate a descriptive caption for this audio.”. Or like OSUM-EChat <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, input the emotion label into the text LLM to obtain empathy information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">However, such work remains largely limited to acoustic information and audio analysis. Unlike vision, audio in speech LLMs is not only an object of analysis but also serves as the query modality in dialogue tasks. For example, VoxEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> evaluates mathematical problem-solving via spoken questions, and VoiceBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> benchmarks speech-to-text dialogue tasks. To this end, we are the first to apply explicit cross-modal semantic knowledge distillation to direct dialogue tasks (text-to-text and speech-to-text), which targets higher-level knowledge transfer and reasoning enhancement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) Experimental validation.</span> Using only about 60,000 samples, our method substantially improves the overall capabilities of speech LLMs such as Qwen2.5-Omni <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. Experimental results show that, after distillation, the model demonstrates clear gains in general ability tests, commonsense reasoning, multidisciplinary knowledge, instruction following, and complex audio analysis and reasoning tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Capability Degradation Evaluation</h2>

<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.14930v1/distillation_Framework_ICASSP.svg" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:357/161;" width="357" height="161"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>An overview of the proposed <span id="S2.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cross-Modal Knowledge Distillation for Speech LLMs</span>, which consists of two complementary channels: <span id="S2.F1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Speech-to-Text (S<math id="S2.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>T)</span> and <span id="S2.F1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Text-to-Text (T<math id="S2.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>T)</span>. Each channel trains the speech LLM using the <math id="S2.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>CE</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math> with either the gold label <math id="S2.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math> or the text LLM-generated label <math id="S2.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, combined with the <math id="S2.F1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math> scaled by the temperature <math id="S2.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Problem Definition</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define the evaluation setting as follows: let the text-form question in a dialogue QA task be <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the corresponding speech-form question be <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>. The text base model is denoted as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the speech large language model as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. Their corresponding outputs are:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T1=\theta_{t}(Q_{t}),\quad T2=\theta_{s}(Q_{t}),\quad T3=\theta_{s}(Q_{a})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T1=\theta_{t}(Q_{t}),\quad T2=\theta_{s}(Q_{t}),\quad T3=\theta_{s}(Q_{a}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The gap between <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T2</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the <span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">catastrophic forgetting</span>, while the performance gap between <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T3</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the <span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">modality inequivalence</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Evaluation Results</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate on the VoiceBench dataset, which provides both speech and text modalities across multiple QA dimensions. The results are shown in Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Cross-Modal Knowledge Distillation for Speech Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Speech LLMs generally suffer from catastrophic forgetting, as their performance under text input is consistently lower than that of their corresponding text base models. For instance, LLaMA-Omni achieves 74.26 under text input, compared with 81.43 for LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These models also exhibit modality inequivalence, with speech input producing weaker results than text input. Although Freeze-Omni avoids catastrophic forgetting by freezing the backbone LLM, it performs poorly under speech input, highlighting a trade-off between preserving text knowledge and achieving cross-modal consistency. Overall, these results reveal that catastrophic forgetting and modality inequivalence are prevalent challenges in current speech LLMs,</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Notation and overview</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We denote the text-form question by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the synthesized speech question by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>. The text-base (teacher) model is <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the speech large language (student) model is <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. For an input <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> (text or speech) the teacher and student produce token-level logits sequences</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{T}=\theta_{t}(Q_{t}),\qquad\mathbf{z}^{S}=\theta_{s}(x)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>S</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{T}=\theta_{t}(Q_{t}),\qquad\mathbf{z}^{S}=\theta_{s}(x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{T}=\{z^{T}_{1},\dots,z^{T}_{T}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{T}=\{z^{T}_{1},\dots,z^{T}_{T}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{S}=\{z^{S}_{1},\dots,z^{S}_{T^{\prime}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>S</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>S</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>S</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{S}=\{z^{S}_{1},\dots,z^{S}_{T^{\prime}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are the logits at each decoding step (lengths <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> may differ). We write the reference (gold) response as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}=(y_{1},\dots,y_{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}=(y_{1},\dots,y_{L})</annotation></semantics></math> and the teacher-generated response as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\hat{y}}=(\hat{y}_{1},\dots,\hat{y}_{\hat{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\hat{y}}=(\hat{y}_{1},\dots,\hat{y}_{\hat{L}})</annotation></semantics></math>. A TTS system <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is used to synthesize speech from text:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{a}=\mathcal{T}(Q_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{a}=\mathcal{T}(Q_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our objective is to distill the teacher’s knowledge into the student through two complementary channels:
(i) <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Text-to-Text (T<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>T) distillation</span>, where the student is trained on textual inputs (<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=Q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=Q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) to mitigate catastrophic forgetting;
(ii) <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Speech-to-Text (S<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>T) distillation</span>, where the student is trained on synthesized speech (<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=Q_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=Q_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>) to align speech-mode outputs with the teacher’s text-mode outputs, reducing modality inequivalence.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Speech-to-Text (S<math id="S3.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>T) evaluation results on VoiceBench. CE denotes cross-entropy training on dataset labels <math id="S3.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, while Teacher CE uses text LLM-generated labels <math id="S3.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> as hard targets.</figcaption>
